## An interface part, cut into layers

Take a modal window. What does it need to be a good one?

1. **Structure:** the markup of the window, a heading, a close button.
2. **Behavior:** it opens on a press, closes on Esc and on a click outside it, keeps focus inside, and gives focus back to the button.
3. **Accessibility:** a screen reader announces the window, and the background is not read out.
4. **Look:** colors, corners, a shadow, an animation.

A beginner thinks about the fourth, but the difficulties lie in the first three: they are hard to get right even by hand. That is why systems differ in **how many layers they take on**. Choose an approach on the diagram and see what is left for you.

<LayerMatrix />

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: what four layers does an interface part have?</summary>

Structure, behavior, accessibility and look.

</details>

## Product systems: Polaris and Primer

**Polaris** is Shopify’s system for the interface of a shop’s admin, and **Primer** is GitHub’s system. Both are made for the company’s own products, and that is both their strength and their weakness.

The strength: they answer in detail the question “how do you build working screens”: lists and details, tables, settings forms. The examples are visible and alive: millions of people use them.

The weakness: outside their own platform they travel badly. Polaris now rests on web components for apps that are embedded in Shopify’s admin. And Primer at GitHub has two faces: Product UI for the interface of the service itself, and Brand UI together with a Brand toolkit for marketing pages. This is an instructive split: an “interface you work in” and a “page that persuades” have different needs for density and air, and the systems for them should be different, or at least have different modes. Primer calls its tokens “primitives”: color, spacing and typography.

<SystemExplorer ids={['polaris', 'primer', 'radix', 'shadcn', 'tailwind']} />

## Headless: Radix and its relatives

**Radix Primitives** (a project of WorkOS) gives complex behaviors with no line of styling: an accordion, a dialog, a dropdown menu, tabs and others, each in its own package. Everything follows the WAI-ARIA guidelines, with keyboard control and focus management that are tested with screen readers. You write the look yourself, in CSS or in Tailwind.

Radix has relatives: Adobe’s React Aria and Headless UI. The idea is the same: **do the hardest and most boring part (accessibility) once, and give it to everyone for free**, and leave the styling to the authors.

But before a library, think about the platform. The browser can do a lot by itself: `details` opens a block, `dialog` holds focus and closes on Esc, the `popover` attribute gives a layer that pops up. All of these are already “headless” components, built into the language. If a task is solved by them, you need no library.

## shadcn/ui: code you own

**shadcn/ui** calls itself “not a component library”: the components are not installed as a package but copied by a command straight into your project as source code. You get both the behavior and a starting look, and you can change anything without waiting for another library’s release. You pay by making the updates and fixes yourself. It is an owner’s choice: “my code, my concern”. The source is open.

## Tailwind: a system inside the markup

**Tailwind CSS** turns the usual order upside down. Instead of a ready component you get a **vocabulary of short classes**, and they are built on the tokens of a theme: `p-4` is a spacing from the spacing scale, `text-lg` is a size from the type scale, `bg-blue-600` is a color from the palette. In Tailwind the theme is described by CSS variables, and each variable produces utility classes.

The main property of such a system: **by default you don’t write “17 pixels”**, you take a value from the vocabulary. There is an escape hatch (a value in square brackets, `p-[17px]`), but it stands out in the markup and counts as an exception. That is the discipline of a design system, built into the very way of writing. The price: the markup grows classes, and accessibility is your responsibility. You already know tokens; in this lesson you will make a small vocabulary of utilities and see how it is built inside.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: how is shadcn/ui different from a library?</summary>

The components’ code is copied into your project and becomes yours: you are free to change it, but you will have to fix it yourself.

</details>

## How to choose

A rough rule:
- **You need it fast and without character** (an internal service): a ready-made system.
- **You need a character of your own and complex widgets:** headless components or shadcn/ui.
- **You need only the look by rules:** utilities.
- **A simple site without complex widgets:** plain HTML, your own tokens and components.

For your project it is almost certainly the last. But it is useful to know the others, because sites and builders that you will meet in the parts on AI are made from exactly these.

## Practice

Build a mini Tailwind from your project’s tokens: eight utility classes and a page built from them alone. Notice how the rule for the look moves from one place (“this is what a card looks like”) into words (“it has spacing 4, a radius and the accent”).

## What was wrong with 2014

In 2014 there was no limited vocabulary: everyone wrote sizes and colors as they liked, and pages drifted apart. Later it turned out that the constraints (“only these values are allowed”) were the most valuable thing in systems, and Tailwind, shadcn/ui and Radix are built on that discovery.
